<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
     FacetUI - Settings.

     The first of the per-app overlays, and the one that sets the pattern for
     the rest.

     WHAT THIS DOES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT

     It aligns Settings' own surfaces with FacetUI's palette, so the app reads
     as part of the system rather than as a separate piece of Material sitting
     inside it.

     It does NOT make those surfaces glass, and that is not an oversight. An
     app's headers, cards and list backgrounds are VIEWS inside the app's single
     window, and Android has no backdrop blur for a view: a view can blur its
     own content, not what is painted beneath it in the same window. Only
     windows can blur what is behind them, which is why dialogs, menus and the
     keyboard could be made glass and an app bar cannot.

     Settings' own dialogs are AppCompat, bundled into the APK rather than
     inherited from the platform, so the framework dialog glass does not reach
     them either. Overriding a bundled AppCompat theme would mean restating
     every item it declares, and its source is not in the tree - it cannot be
     read, so it cannot be restated safely, so it is left alone. Colour-only is
     the honest ceiling here.

     Resources named below come from two places that both compile into this
     APK: Settings' own res, and SettingsLib, which lives in frameworks/base.
     tools/validate-overlays.py checks both.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="dev.octagonos.facetui.settings">
    <application android:hasCode="false" />
    <overlay
        android:targetPackage="com.android.settings"
        android:isStatic="true"
        android:priority="900" />
</manifest>
